          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Why is it hard to keep taking a preventer inhaler every day when you feel fine?",
          "answer": "Feeling fine is one of the main reasons people stop, but the material I could retrieve doesn't explain that directly. It mostly covers inhaler technique.\n\n**What the sources say:**\n\n- **Technique can quietly reduce the dose.** The asthma adherence guidance (*Sticking with asthma treatment: inhaler technique, controllers and action plans*) says an inhaler used every day with poor technique can deliver much less medicine than intended. Technique errors are common, often last for years unless someone checks, and are usually the first thing reviewed when treatment seems to be failing. Pressurized inhalers and dry powder inhalers each have their own common mistakes. If you use both types, the instructions for each can conflict, so that is worth raising with a clinician.\n- **Cost can cause unnoticed skipping.** A diabetes self-management guide (*Sticking with a diabetes regimen*) notes that cost-related underuse, such as skipping doses or not filling prescriptions, is common and is rarely mentioned unless someone asks. It is not about asthma specifically, but the point applies generally.\n- **Routines help.** The same diabetes guide suggests tying doses to an existing daily habit rather than a clock time, and using reminders or organizers. Again, this is general advice rather than asthma guidance.\n\n**What the sources don't cover:** I did not find anything on why symptom-free people tend to stop controller inhalers, or on how an asthma action plan fits into daily use. The asthma document lists action plans in its title, but the passage I received did not cover them.\n\nIf you find it hard to keep up, a clinician can check your technique, look at cost or routine barriers, and explain what your treatment is for when you feel well. Please don't change or stop a prescribed inhaler on your own.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
